    commType: "technical-explanation",
    commTitle: "Explaining Clearing Accounts vs Reconciliation Accounts in SAP",
    commScenario: "A junior consultant confuses the difference between a sub-ledger Reconciliation Account and a balance sheet Clearing Account.",
    commPrompt: "Explain the distinction: Reconciliation Accounts (e.g., Accounts Payable) are non-directly postable GL accounts that mirror sub-ledgers; Clearing Accounts (e.g., GR/IR, Bank In-transit) are temporary transit accounts that balance to zero.",
    commVocab: ["Reconciliation Accounts (Sub-ledger Mirror)", "Clearing Accounts (Zero-Balance Transit)", "Direct Posting Prohibitions"],
    commRule: "Foundational Clarity (Differentiate permanent sub-ledger summary control from temporary operational transit buckets)",
    commWeakResponse: "Reconciliation accounts are for vendors and customers. Clearing accounts are for everything else in between.",
    commWeakCritique: "Too simplistic and technically incomplete. Misses the vital operational rules governing direct postings and clearing status.",
    commStrongResponse: "This is one of the most essential concepts in SAP finance! A Reconciliation Account acts as a real-time summary mirror of a sub-ledger (such as Accounts Payable or Accounts Receivable). You can never post directly to a reconciliation account in transaction FB50; instead, every time you post to an individual Vendor or Customer master record, SAP automatically updates the underlying Reconciliation Account in the General Ledger simultaneously. Conversely, a Clearing Account (such as the GR/IR account or Bank In-transit account) is a temporary operational holding bucket where two offsetting business events occur at different times. The balance represents timing differences, and when both events complete, the items are cleared against each other to zero.",
    commStrongCritique: "Exceptional pedagogical clarity: contrasts sub-ledger automation with temporal offset holding buckets.",
